+IP sample input mode
+--------------------
+
+IP mode generates line-level DWARF sample profiles on systems without usable
+LBR, SPE, or ETM traces. It is explicitly enabled by ``--ip-samples``; ordinary
+IP input without this option retains the original error behavior. IP counts
+are sampled event weights, **not execution counts**. This mode does not infer
+branch histories, indirect-call targets, or workload representativeness.
+
+Collection and export
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Use a binary built with matching DWARF, a build ID, and profiling discriminators
+(for example ``-gline-tables-only -fdebug-info-for-profiling
+-funique-internal-linkage-names -Wl,--build-id=sha1``). Do not use
+``-fpseudo-probe-for-profiling``: presence of either ``.pseudo_probe`` or
+``.pseudo_probe_desc`` in either runtime or debug file is rejected, even with
+``--use-dwarf-correlation``. This experimental mode has not admitted probe
+correlation or DWARF correlation of probe builds.
+
+Record and export on the recording system with the same perf version::
+
+  perf record --buildid-mmap -e cycles -c 1000003 -o perf.data -- ./workload
+  perf evlist -v -i perf.data
+  LC_ALL=C perf script -i perf.data --show-mmap-events --show-lost-events -F pid,tid,event,period,ip,dsoff > ip.perfscript
+  perf buildid-list -i perf.data > buildids.txt
+
+The evlist output is collection evidence, not an input to llvm-profgen. A
+frequency such as ``-F 1000`` may replace ``-c 1000003``. ``cpu-clock`` is an
+alternative event where hardware cycles are unavailable. Preserve the full
+field list, including ``event``: explicit ``-F`` replaces perf's default
+fields. Do not append call stacks or branch stacks. This command and its
+strict grammar correspond to the IP collection/export contract, sections
+5.2.1 and 5.2.2. The expected perf v6.12 sample spelling is::
+
+  123/123 1000003 cycles: 1234 (/path/to/program+0x234)
+
+The actual printed order is pid/tid, period, complete event name, IP, DSO plus
+file offset. Paths can contain spaces, parentheses, and internal ``+0x``;
+only a final valid hexadecimal ``+0xOFFSET`` is separated. A deleted suffix is
+part of the path before the offset. MMAP, MMAP2, and LOST records are accepted
+in that export's form. Unknown records, extra stack fields, malformed numbers,
+truncated records, and zero sample periods are errors. PID/TID -1 means unknown;
+values below -1 are invalid. Kernel and module samples are counted but do not
+produce kernel profiles; special DSOs are classified separately.
+
+``--perfdata`` is not admitted in IP mode. Export text on the recording system
+instead; matching perf version numbers alone do not establish cross-host
+export equivalence. Example conversion::
+
+  llvm-profgen --ip-samples --binary=workload --perfscript=ip.perfscript \
+    --perf-buildid-list=buildids.txt --ip-aggregation=sum \
+    --format=extbinary --output=workload.prof --quality-report=quality.json
+
+IP options
+~~~~~~~~~~
+
+These options require ``--ip-samples``; they are not an implicit fallback from
+LBR processing. Unknown enum values are diagnosed by the command-line parser.
+
+.. option:: --ip-samples
+
+  Explicitly select IP text input; default off.
+
+.. option:: --ip-event=<name>
+
+  Select the complete printed event name, including modifiers such as
+  ``cycles:u``. Unset by default. Multiple events without this option, or an
+  empty selection, are errors. Different event units are never summed.
+
+.. option:: --ip-weight=[period|sample]
+
+  Default ``period``. Use period sums normalized by P_ref, or sample counts.
+
+.. option:: --ip-period-unit=<N>
+
+  Explicit positive uint64 P_ref. Otherwise use the lower median of periods
+  of target samples, before symbolization and skid adjustment. It is an error to combine
+  this option with ``--ip-weight=sample``. To merge profiles, explicitly use
+  the same normalization unit across recordings.
+
+.. option:: --ip-aggregation=[sum|max|avg]
+
+  **No default during evaluation; must be explicitly specified.** This is a
+  transitional interface: a default must be decided before upstream release.
+
+.. option:: --ip-head-samples=[none|entry-estimate]
+
+  Default ``none`` (provisional): write zero head samples. ``entry-estimate``
+  uses the earliest body position or inline callsite estimate; a sampled
+  function with positive total has a minimum estimate of one. Function total
+  is never substituted for entry count. With ``none``, keep profi enabled
+  during compilation and linking, explicitly enabling it at the LTO link step.
+
+.. option:: --ip-zero-fill=[none|sampled-functions]
+
+  Default ``none`` (provisional). ``sampled-functions`` writes zero at
+  unsampled keys within top-level functions having usable samples. It does not
+  introduce functions without usable samples or change totals.
+
+.. option:: --ip-skid=[none|prev-inst]
+
+  Default ``none`` (provisional). ``prev-inst`` uses the preceding instruction
+  only in the same DWARF range and mapping-state interval, with adjacent bytes
+  and usable predecessor line information. Otherwise retain the original PC
+  and count an unadjusted sample. Normalization uses original sample periods.
+
+.. option:: --binary-match-regex=<regex>
+
+  Unset by default. Search the raw DSO path, including `` (deleted)``; this is
+  not a whole-string match, so use ``^`` and ``$`` to anchor one. A supplied
+  regex completely replaces basename selection. Otherwise match the basename
+  of the realpath of ``--binary``, ignoring a final deleted suffix for matching
+  only. Preserve the original path for identity evidence.
+
+.. option:: --perf-buildid-list=<file>
+
+  Optional buildid-list text exported on the recording system. Supplies
+  path-level identity evidence, not per-sample binding.
+
+.. option:: --allow-missing-build-id
+
+  Default off. Allow missing evidence as ``unverified``; never allow a known
+  mismatch or ambiguous candidates.
+
+.. option:: --quality-report=<file>
+
+  Optional deterministic JSON, schema version 1, described below. Failures
+  also produce failed reports subject to the output contract.
+
+.. option:: --ip-min-usable-ratio=<ratio>
+
+  Optional minimum raw usable/target weight ratio. No default during evaluation.
+  Accept only ``0``, ``1``, or ``0.`` followed by one to nine decimal digits.
+  Compare exact integer fractions, before aggregation, duplication or rounding.
+
+.. option:: --ip-min-target-samples=<N>
+
+  Optional minimum target sample count (decimal uint64). No default during
+  evaluation. Zero target or usable samples remains an independent error.
+
+.. option:: --ip-max-lost-ratio=<ratio>
+
+  Optional maximum visible LOST diagnostic ratio, with the same exact syntax
+  as the usable ratio. No default during evaluation. Unknown ratios do not
+  fail this check. A uint64 denominator overflow produces ``unknown`` and
+  ``W_LOST_RATIO_OVERFLOW``, not a conversion failure.
+
+Existing options
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The following retain their meaning: ``--binary``, ``--debug-binary``, ``--dwp``,
+``--output``/``-o``, ``--format``, ``--use-md5``, ``--pid``,
+``--show-mmap-events``, ``--show-detailed-warning``, ``--trim-cold-profile``,
+``--profile-density-*``, ``--show-density``, ``--load-function-from-symbol``,
+and ``--use-dwarf-correlation`` (which does not bypass probe rejection).
+``--pid`` selects that process, not children after fork, and never matches -1.
+Negative ``--pid`` is rejected. IP output supports text, binary, and extbinary;
+GCC format fails at writer creation. ``--use-md5`` applies only to extbinary
+and is otherwise ignored with a warning. ``--show-mmap-events`` prints parsed
+IP mappings, without claiming samples were independently rebound to them.
+
+Explicit use of any of these is rejected: ``--unsymbolized-profile``,
+``--llvm-sample-profile``, ``--skip-symbolization``, ``--data-access-perftrace``,
+``--ignore-stack-samples``, ``--csprof-*``, ``--csspgo-*``,
+``--use-context-cost-for-preinliner``, ``--max-search-depth``,
+``--gen-cs-nested-profile``, ``--compress-recursion``,
+``--mark-all-context-preinlined``, ``--use-offset``,
+``--use-first-loadable-segment-as-base``, ``--update-total-samples``,
+``--fill-zero-for-all-funcs``, ``--populate-profile-symbol-list``, ``--kernel``,
+``--show-disassembly-only``, ``--disassemble-functions``,
+``--show-source-locations``, ``--show-canonical-fname``, ``--show-pseudo-probe``,
+and ``--infer-missing-frames`` (even explicitly specifying its default).
+``--output=-`` and IP ``--perfdata`` are also rejected.
+
+Identity and address conversion
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+A file offset must be in exactly one executable PT_LOAD file range. Convert
+using ``p_vaddr + (offset - p_offset)``; do not use a process base or page-size
+mask. Segment gaps are unmapped-in-target. Arithmetic overflow is an error.
+Identity is conservative across all candidate paths:
+
+* ``verified``: every required executable candidate mapping has matching
+  per-mapping build-ID evidence.
+* ``verified-path``: matching path-level evidence covers candidates, but
+  per-mapping evidence is incomplete.
+* ``unverified``: necessary evidence is missing and explicitly allowed.
+* ``mismatch``: a known ID conflict, asymmetric runtime/debug identity, or
+  ambiguous candidates. This is never bypassed.
+
+A non-executable mapping with a known ID conflict still causes mismatch; a
+non-executable mapping with no ID is not required evidence. A candidate with
+no executable mapping remains unverified. For a deleted path, buildid-list
+cannot substitute for a missing mapping ID.
+
+Runtime-with-ID/input-without-evidence is missing, not mismatch; the reverse
+is mismatch. Runtime/debug IDs must agree, including presence. Probe sections
+in either file are rejected. Paths with differing device/inode tuples are
+ambiguous. Generation zero means unknown: only unequal nonzero generations
+conflict. Inode reuse cannot be detected if one generation is unknown.
+Mapping-start and synthesized mapping IDs are not distinguished in text;
+path-level evidence does not prove when an image was read, nor does it bind
+each sample independently to a particular mapping.
+
+Counting and instruction boundaries
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+For an address a, u(a) is sample_count or period_sum. Sample weighting uses
+P_ref=1; automatic period normalization uses target periods before symbolization,
+not only samples that later have line information. A key contains the full
+inline chain, relative leaf line, and base discriminator. Sum computes
+``sum(u(a)) / P_ref``; max computes ``max(u(a)*duplication(a)) / P_ref``;
+avg computes ``sum(u(a)*duplication(a)) / (P_ref*n(key))``. The avg denominator
+includes every instruction with the same key, including unsampled instructions
+and unrolled copies across all function ranges, excluding data and line zero.
+Each key is divided once and rounded half up; a sampled key is at least one.
+Checked integer arithmetic never wraps. Function totals sum normalized raw
+sample weights independently of body aggregation; inline ancestors receive
+those weights. The body sums need not equal total.
+
+Only avg, sampled-functions zero filling, or prev-inst requires instruction
+boundaries. Sum/max with none/none does not enumerate instructions. Enumeration
+is limited to functions with usable samples. It uses local STT_NOTYPE mapping
+symbols from the runtime and optional matching debug file .symtab, matched by
+section name and address range, never section index. ARM uses ``$a/$t/$d``;
+AArch64 uses ``$x/$d``; dot-suffixed variants are recognized. Runtime bytes are
+required even when debug code sections are NOBITS. No mapping state crosses
+section boundaries, and same-address conflicting states fail.
+
+A32/A64 instructions start on four-byte boundaries. Little-endian ELF32 Thumb
+is stepped forward from the current mapping anchor: a first halfword >=0xe800
+means four bytes, otherwise two. Data ranges are excluded, even if decodable.
+A DWARF intersection may not start inside or end halfway through an instruction.
+A prefix read to prove the first boundary is not part of the profile. Samples
+not at proven instruction starts fail rather than being rounded. All failed
+functions are reported; no partial profile or silent fallback is produced.
+
+Output and failure contract
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Before ELF loading or any temporary file, reject stdout output and any
+profile/report alias, including aliases to perfscript, binary, debug-binary,
+buildid-list or dwp inputs. Relative names, symlinks and hard links count.
+**Path conflict is the sole intentional no-failure-report exception**: neither
+output is written, all existing bytes are retained, and stderr explains it.
+
+For other failures, the old profile is always retained. Write and explicitly
+flush/close/check a same-directory profile temporary, atomically replace the
+quality report via its own checked temporary, then rename the profile last.
+Successful reports replace old reports; ordinary failures replace them with
+complete failed JSON. If the report itself cannot be written or committed,
+keep the old report and remove its temporary. If profile commit fails, try to
+replace the report with a failed report. No fallible operation follows a
+successful profile commit. This is not a two-file crash-atomic transaction;
+nonzero exit never authorizes use of a new profile. Disk failure, process
+crash, and LLVM fatal errors can prevent delivery of a report.
+
+IP errors return through the IP driver; existing shared loader/symbolizer exits
+have a one-shot stage-aware callback. Ordinary command-line parser diagnostics
+are emitted once, verbatim, and recorded as the parse-stage reason. A missing
+required IP binary or negative PID has its IP-specific diagnostic.
+
+Quality report schema (version 1)
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+This unreleased schema keeps version 1. An unavailable or uncomputed value is
+the string ``"unknown"``. Confirmed absence is JSON ``null`` (not an empty
+string), including no build ID, no debug binary, or no failure on success.
+An empty argv token is represented by null, retaining its position. Empty
+arrays mean a known empty list unless a field's definition explicitly says
+otherwise; a whole uncomputed object or list is unknown. Zero is a known numeric
+zero, not an unavailable value. JSON integers are exact uint64 (signed int64
+for PID/TID), never floating point. Fractions are objects with integer
+``numerator`` and ``denominator``; zero or unrepresentable denominators are
+unknown. Count weights retain raw numerator and P_ref without rounding.
+
+Arrays are deterministic: event/candidate names sort lexicographically;
+mapping/evidence records retain input order; top lists sort by selected raw
+weight descending, then name ascending. Boundary sources use sorted file names,
+failed functions use sorted names, and warnings use stable code order. Argument
+order is preserved. No timestamps or performance benefit claims are emitted.
+
+Let T=target, U=original usable, N=in-function without line information, L=sum
+of visible LOST N values, S=all pre-filter sample lines. Selected raw weight W
+is sample_count in sample mode and period_sum otherwise. Ratio definitions:
+
+* target_usable_ratio = W(U)/W(T); the sample/period alternatives use their
+  named counters. These precede skid, duplication, aggregation and trimming.
+* line_resolution_ratio = W(U)/(W(U)+W(N)).
+* dwarf_function_ratio = (U.sample_count+N.sample_count)/T.sample_count.
+* aarch64_alignment_ratio = proven aligned target samples/T.sample_count.
+* ISA sample_ratio and weight_ratio use T as denominator; unmapped target
+  offsets are not assigned an ISA, so the ratios need not sum to one.
+* Top shares use other_dso for other_dso_top20, target for outside_symtab_top20,
+  usable for coverage top50 and name_merged_top50.
+* exported_lost_ratio = L/(L+S), count-based and input-wide. LOST may include
+  sideband records and cannot be assigned to one event, DSO or PID. Its
+  completeness is always unknown. No visible LOST record means unknown L and
+  ratio, not zero loss. L+S overflow yields unknown plus a warning.
+
+Threshold precedence is: unset -> not_evaluated; set but unavailable or
+undefined -> unknown (warn, do not fail this check); otherwise compare exactly.
+Usable/sample comparisons are passed or failed; LOST is exceeded or
+not_exceeded. sampling_adequacy is insufficient only on failed minimum-sample
+or exceeded LOST checks, otherwise unknown, never ok. Independent failures
+(identity, empty target/usable, malformed input, boundaries, writer) still fail
+regardless of unset/unknown thresholds. conversion is ok or failed;
+failure_stage is load, parse, identity, generate, boundary, write or commit.
+On success failure_stage/reason are null. Other verdicts need not have been
+computed when an earlier independent failure prevents them.
+
+The field inventory below uses dot paths and ``[]`` for array elements.
+An object-valued field may instead be unknown when its prerequisite is absent.
+Count objects have sample_count (samples), period_sum (selected event units),
+and normalized_weight (raw selected weight/P_ref). Top entries contain name,
+counts, and share as defined above. Boundary failures contain function and
+reason; evidence items contain path, source, build_id, result and optional
+mapping_index (unknown when not applicable).
+
+Field paths
+^^^^^^^^^^^
+
+``boundaries`` (object)
+  On-demand enumeration observations.
+
+``boundaries.failures`` (array)
+  Failed functions and reasons, sorted by function name; empty if none collected yet.
+
+``boundaries.failures[]`` (object)
+  Array element with the child fields below; parent ordering and absence rules apply.
+
+``boundaries.failures[].function`` (string)
+  DWARF function name whose boundary enumeration failed.
+
+``boundaries.failures[].reason`` (string)
+  Description of the failed boundary condition, with available function/address context.
+
+``boundaries.function_count`` (string | uint64)
+  Number of enumerated functions; unknown before generation starts.
+
+``boundaries.requested`` (boolean)
+  Whether avg, zero filling or skid requests enumeration; false before validated semantics.
+
+``boundaries.sources`` (array)
+  Sorted distinct mapping-symbol source paths; empty if none collected yet.
+
+``boundaries.sources[]`` (string)
+  Array element with the child fields below; parent ordering and absence rules apply.
+
+``consumer_hints`` (array)
+  Compiler-use recommendations in fixed order, not runtime benefit claims.
+
+``consumer_hints[]`` (string)
+  Text recommendation; keep profi enabled when head estimation is disabled.
+
+``coverage`` (object)
+  Sampled function and address coverage; uncomputed children unknown.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_functions`` (string | uint64)
+  Number of sampled names sourced from more than one DWARF DIE start.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50`` (array | string)
+  Top 50 multi-DIE sampled names, same weight/name order; share denominator is all usable weight.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[]`` (object)
+  Array element with the child fields below; parent ordering and absence rules apply.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].counts`` (object)
+  Count object for the parent bucket; raw samples/periods and normalization.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].counts.normalized_weight`` (object)
+  Exact selected raw weight divided by P_ref; unknown until P_ref is known.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].counts.normalized_weight.denominator`` (uint64)
+  Positive integer denominator of the parent fraction (see ratio/weight definitions above).
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].counts.normalized_weight.numerator`` (uint64)
+  Exact integer numerator of the parent fraction, in its stated raw units.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].counts.period_sum`` (uint64)
+  Sum of periods in the parent bucket (that event's units).
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].counts.sample_count`` (uint64)
+  Number of samples in the parent bucket (samples).
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].name`` (string)
+  Event, DSO or function name; unresolved symbol names are unknown.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].share`` (object)
+  Selected original weight divided by the parent list's stated denominator.
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].share.denominator`` (uint64)
+  Positive integer denominator of the parent fraction (see ratio/weight definitions above).
+
+``coverage.name_merged_top50[].share.numerator`` (uint64)
+  Exact integer numerator of the parent fraction, in its stated raw units.
+
+``coverage.sampled_functions`` (string | uint64)
+  Number of top-level names with usable samples (after existing name merging).
+
+``coverage.top50`` (array | string)
+  Top 50 sampled functions by selected original usable weight, then name.
+
+``coverage.top50[]`` (object)
+  Array element with the child fields below; parent ordering and absence rules apply.
+
+``coverage.top50[].counts`` (object)
+  Count object for the parent bucket; raw samples/periods and normalization.
+
+``coverage.top50[].counts.normalized_weight`` (object)
+  Exact selected raw weight divided by P_ref; unknown until P_ref is known.
+
+``coverage.top50[].counts.normalized_weight.denominator`` (uint64)
+  Positive integer denominator of the parent fraction (see ratio/weight definitions above).
+
+``coverage.top50[].counts.normalized_weight.numerator`` (uint64)
+  Exact integer numerator of the parent fraction, in its stated raw units.
+
+``coverage.top50[].counts.period_sum`` (uint64)
+  Sum of periods in the parent bucket (that event's units).
+
+``coverage.top50[].counts.sample_count`` (uint64)
+  Number of samples in the parent bucket (samples).
+
+``coverage.top50[].name`` (string)
+  Event, DSO or function name; unresolved symbol names are unknown.
+
+``coverage.top50[].share`` (object)
+  Selected original weight divided by the parent list's stated denominator.
+
+``coverage.top50[].share.denominator`` (uint64)
+  Positive integer denominator of the parent fraction (see ratio/weight definitions above).
+
+``coverage.top50[].share.numerator`` (uint64)
+  Exact integer numerator of the parent fraction, in its stated raw units.
+
+``coverage.unique_addresses`` (string | uint64)
+  Number of distinct successfully converted target virtual addresses, including later unusable addresses.
+
+``event`` (object)
+  Event selection and independent per-event counts.
+
+``event.before_filter`` (array | string)
+  Per-event pre-filter counts; lexicographic full event-name order. Unknown before parsing completes.
+
+``event.before_filter[]`` (object)
+  Array element with the child fields below; parent ordering and absence rules apply.
+
+``event.before_filter[].name`` (string)
+  Event, DSO or function name; unresolved symbol names are unknown.
+
+``event.before_filter[].period_sum`` (uint64)
+  Sum of periods in the parent bucket (that event's units).
+
+``event.before_filter[].sample_count`` (uint64)
+  Number of samples in the parent bucket (samples).
+
+``event.config`` (string)
+  Unknown; not inferred from the event name.
+
+``event.selected`` (string)
+  Complete selected event name, including modifiers; unknown before selection.
+
+``event.type`` (string)
+  Unknown; not inferred from the event name.
+
+``identity`` (object)
+  Target and debug identity chain.
+
+``identity.binary`` (string)
+  Given runtime path, null if omitted.
+
+``identity.binary_build_id`` (null | string)
+  Hex ID, null if read and absent, unknown if not yet inspected.
+
+``identity.binary_realpath`` (string)
+  Canonical runtime path, unknown until resolved.
+
+``identity.candidates`` (array | string)
+  Selected original paths, lexicographic order; unknown before selection.
+
+``identity.candidates[]`` (object)
+  Array element with the child fields below; parent ordering and absence rules apply.
+
+``identity.candidates[].deleted`` (boolean)
+  Whether the original path ends in the deleted suffix.
+
+``identity.candidates[].path`` (string)
+  Unmodified input path; null only if absent.
+
+``identity.debug_binary`` (null | string)
+  Given debug path, null if omitted.
+
+``identity.debug_build_id`` (null | string)
+  Hex ID; null if no debug input or ID inspected and absent; unknown if given but not inspected.
+
+``identity.evidence`` (array | string)
+  Evidence in observation order, unknown before runtime identity read.
+
+``identity.evidence[]`` (object)
+  Array element with the child fields below; parent ordering and absence rules apply.
+
+``identity.evidence[].build_id`` (null | string)
+  Hexadecimal build ID, null for confirmed absence.
+
+``identity.evidence[].mapping_index`` (string | uint64)
+  Zero-based parsed mapping index; unknown for non-mapping evidence.
+
+``identity.evidence[].path`` (string)
+  Unmodified input path; null only if absent.
+
+``identity.evidence[].result`` (string)
+  consistent, mismatch or missing for ID reads; mapping/path-summary grades verified, verified-path, unverified, mismatch; not-required for a non-executable mapping without conflict.
+
+``identity.evidence[].source`` (string)
+  debug-binary, buildid-list, mmap2, missing, or path-summary; interpreted with result.
+
+``identity.grade`` (string)
+  Conservative verified, verified-path, unverified, mismatch, or unknown.
+
+``identity.observed_mmap_build_id`` (boolean | string)
+  Whether any parsed mapping has an ID, not just candidates; unknown before full parsing.
+
+``identity.selection`` (string)
+  regex or basename; selection mechanism, not an identity proof.
+
+``input`` (object)
